\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

When multilingual large language models (LLMs) encounter conflicting information across languages, the language of presentation can determine which answer the model produces \citep{zhao2026when}.
Yet the internal mechanism remains opaque: how does the model encode the conflict signal, can it be detected from internal representations, and does the detected signal causally influence resolution?

Existing work on probing conflict signals has been limited to monolingual settings \citep{zhao2024analysing, brink2026probing}.
Extending cross-lingually introduces confounds (translation quality, token overlap, general transfer properties), and probing establishes correlation rather than causation \citep{hewitt2019designing}; \citet{basu2026interpretability} showed that near-perfect probe accuracy does not guarantee effective steering.

Our work extends \citet{zhao2024analysing} in four directions: (1)~cross-lingual expansion from monolingual English to EN-ZH conflict probing; (2)~a systematic confound exclusion pipeline; (3)~the XNLI baseline revealing that asymmetry parallels general cross-lingual structure; and (4)~three-model causal intervention demonstrating that detectability does not predict causal relevance.
We combine residual stream probing with representation steering across Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Qwen3-8B, and Gemma-2-9B-IT.
Probes achieve 77.6\%, 75.0\%, and 76.2\% balanced accuracy on English conflict detection, and all cross-lingual transfer directions exceed chance (binomial $p < 0.001$).
Cross-lingual transfer exhibits a directional asymmetry (ZH$\to$EN $>$ EN$\to$ZH by ${\sim}$7~pp) that is consistent with the XNLI non-conflict baseline \citep{conneau2018xnli}, reflecting general cross-lingual structure rather than conflict-specific encoding.
Our causal analysis reveals a dissociation in Llama: the probe signal peaks at L28 (88\% depth), but steering succeeds only at L13 (41\% depth; Fisher $p = 0.002$), with all late-layer interventions producing no significant change.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_pipeline.pdf}
\caption{Overview of the cross-lingual conflict probing pipeline. We construct parallel EN/ZH conflict data from ConflictQA with COMET-validated translations, train per-layer logistic regression probes in four transfer directions, and apply three confound exclusion controls (COMET tercile ablation, token overlap analysis, and XNLI non-conflict baseline).}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Cross-lingual conflict detectability.} First evidence that knowledge conflict signals are linearly detectable across languages, with all transfer directions exceeding chance (binomial $p < 0.001$).
    \item \textbf{Asymmetry reflects general cross-lingual structure.} The transfer asymmetry (ZH$\to$EN $>$ EN$\to$ZH by ${\sim}$7~pp) matches the XNLI non-conflict baseline, establishing that it reflects general cross-lingual alignment rather than conflict-specific encoding.
    \item \textbf{Detection--decision dissociation.} Linear probe peak (L28), MLP probe peak (L18), and causal steering node (L13; Fisher $p = 0.002$) diverge in Llama, demonstrating that detectability does not predict causal relevance. Steering fails in Qwen and Gemma despite high probe accuracy; unlike prior behavioral observations \citep{basu2026interpretability}, we provide mechanistic layer-level localization.
    \item \textbf{Confound exclusion pipeline.} COMET translation quality ablation, token overlap analysis, and non-conflict baseline comparison exclude common confounds in cross-lingual probing.
\end{itemize}

